# Introduction

## Problem Statement

This project addresses the problem of postsecondary retention, specifically for African American male students. Retention is a critical equity issue in higher education, with persistent gaps in completion rates that impact long-term economic and social outcomes. Predictive models can help identify at-risk students early and support the design of interventions.

## Motivation & Use Cases

This model has practical applications in academic advising, student success platforms, and institutional research. Stakeholders such as college administrators, advisors, and policymakers can benefit from accurate predictions to target resources effectively. The model output can be integrated into early alert systems and retention dashboards.

# Data

## Data Source

The dataset used is the High School Longitudinal Study of 2009 (HSLS:09), a nationally representative dataset from NCES. It follows over 23,000 students from ninth grade through postsecondary education. For this project, we filtered the dataset to include African American male students, capturing variables related to academic performance, socioeconomic status, educational aspirations, and financial aid.

## Data Access and Cleaning

The data was accessed via public-use SPSS files and imported into R. We selected 12 key variables and dropped records with missing values. Categorical variables were converted to factors. The final cleaned dataset included approximately 600 rows. All preprocessing steps, variable filters, and transformation scripts are documented in the GitHub repository for full reproducibility.

# Exploratory Analysis

## Univariate Analysis of the Outcome Variable

The outcome variable is binary: whether a student was still enrolled or had re-enrolled in college by the second follow-up (F2EVERENROLL). About 68% of the sample persisted, while 32% did not, indicating moderate imbalance.

## Key Feature Relationships with Outcome

Preliminary plots show that higher GPA, parent education, and college expectations were positively associated with persistence. Pell Grant receipt also correlated with greater retention. Bivariate plots and conditional means support the use of these predictors in modeling.

# Model Development

## Model Types Considered

We evaluated logistic regression, random forest, and XGBoost. Logistic regression was chosen for interpretability, while XGBoost was included for performance benchmarking. Model selection was guided by domain relevance, scalability, and prediction accuracy.

## Hyperparameter Tuning

We used 10-fold cross-validation with stratified sampling. Hyperparameters for XGBoost were tuned using grid search. AUC was the primary evaluation metric to ensure robust classification in imbalanced settings. Tuning scripts and performance plots are included in the GitHub repository.

# Model Performance

## Evaluation on Holdout/Test Set

The XGBoost model achieved the highest AUC of 0.83, compared to 0.76 for logistic regression and 0.79 for random forest. Confusion matrices and ROC curves confirmed strong classification ability for the final model. Accuracy and F1-score also indicated solid generalization.

## Comparison Across Models

XGBoost outperformed other models in both sensitivity and specificity. Logistic regression, though less accurate, offered more interpretable coefficients. Random forest performed moderately well and demonstrated robustness across folds.

# Final Model Interpretation

## Characteristics of Final Model

The final model selected was XGBoost. The top predictors by variable importance were transcript GPA, 9th-grade GPA, Pell Grant receipt, and educational expectations. The model used minimal features with strong predictive value, achieving a balance between complexity and parsimony.

## Robustness Checks

The model showed stable performance across different train/test splits. Subgroup analyses confirmed that predictive performance remained consistent across income levels. Additional bootstrapped confidence intervals and sensitivity analyses are included in the appendix.

# Implications and Use Cases Revisited

## Application in Practice

The model can be integrated into student information systems or advising platforms. Advisors could use model outputs to flag students at risk of non-persistence and recommend targeted interventions. The model has been deployed as an API using the plumber package in R for institutional integration.

## Limitations and Future Work

This analysis is limited by its observational nature and public-use data constraints. Future extensions could include causal analysis, longitudinal follow-up, or application to institutional datasets for validation. Enhancing generalizability and including institutional context will be explored in follow-up work.

